=== Iteration 11 — 2026-08-19T10:05Z ===
Task: FR-16 (PRD.md §4.4) — unit test for TrustedContacts.is_trusted() covering exact match,
substring match, case-insensitivity, and a non-matching sender being rejected.

Read the class directly and found a real, easy-to-miss behavior worth testing explicitly:
is_trusted() returns TRUE BY DEFAULT when the allowlist is completely empty (no contacts
added yet at all) -- documented in its own docstring as a deliberate "don't silently go quiet
with no indication why" choice. Only once at least one contact exists does it become a real
allowlist. The task's "non-matching sender rejected" case only holds once the list is
non-empty -- tested both states explicitly, not just the non-empty case.

Wrote app/src/main/python/tests/test_trusted_contacts.py, 8 tests:
1. Empty list -> is_trusted() True for literally anyone (the documented default).
2. Exact match.
3. Substring match (trusted "mom" matches "Mom", "Mom [heart emoji]", "Call me Mom").
4. Case-insensitivity.
5. Non-matching sender rejected once the list is non-empty (both a clearly unrelated name and
   an empty string).
6. add() normalizes (strip + lowercase) and dedupes via INSERT OR IGNORE -- "  Mom  " and
   "MOM" both added -> list_all() has exactly one entry, "mom".
7. add() with an empty/whitespace-only name is a no-op -- AND confirms this didn't create a
   phantom empty-string entry that would match everything via substring-in-string (would be a
   real, subtle bug if it had).
8. remove() takes a contact off the list; is_trusted() correctly flips to False for that
   sender afterward while a remaining contact still matches.

